<!--
  Empty-track outline for the battery gauge: a fill-based capsule annulus (not a stroke),
  tinted at runtime via ColorFilter exactly like ic_dot_ring. The interior is genuinely
  transparent, so a translucent-background widget shows the wallpaper through the hollow —
  a nested opaque "hole" can't do that (it just reveals the pill behind it).

  Sizing matches the *visible* dot, not its cell box. ic_dot_filled draws a radius-6 circle in
  a 24-unit viewport, so a dot rendered in a `dot`-sized box only shows a dot/2-tall circle;
  the gauge is therefore rendered at height == dot/2. The off-dot ring rim is 1.5/24 of that
  box == dot/16. To land the same dot/16 rim at a dot/2-tall gauge, the vector rim is 1/8 of
  its height (1 unit in 8): (dot/2)/8 == dot/16. Gauge and dots share one outline weight.

  Viewport aspect (139:8 ≈ 17.4) matches the gauge track's fixed aspect
  (trackWidth/gaugeHeight = (5 - (1-DOT_CELL_FRACTION)/2)/(DOT_CELL_FRACTION/2) ≈ 17.36 with
  DOT_CELL_FRACTION = 0.55 and gaugeHeight = dot/2). Because the two match, FillBounds scales
  the vector near-uniformly and the rim stays even all the way around. If DOT_CELL_FRACTION or
  the gaugeHeight == dot/2 relationship changes, re-derive this aspect or the rim will thicken
  on the caps.

  evenOdd carves the inner capsule out of the outer one. Both are full capsules (corner
  radius = half height), so the straight vertical edges are zero-length and the caps are
  clean semicircles.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="139dp"
    android:height="8dp"
    android:viewportWidth="139"
    android:viewportHeight="8">
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M4,0 L135,0 A4,4 0 0 1 135,8 L4,8 A4,4 0 0 1 4,0 Z M4,1 L135,1 A3,3 0 0 1 135,7 L4,7 A3,3 0 0 1 4,1 Z" />
</vector>
